course: dictionary
lesson: 11802
title: Besieging: surrounding and crowding
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Besieging means surrounding a place so that people cannot get in or out. In history, a besieging army camps outside the walls of a town or castle and waits. «The besieging army stayed outside the castle for six weeks.» The clue is the army waiting outside: they want the castle to give up.
explain: Besieging is the -ing form of besiege. The other forms are besieges, besieged and besiege. «The soldiers besieged the fort all winter.» Besieged is the past form, so the soldiers did it in the past.
explain: The word also has an everyday meaning. «Fans were besieging the stars' hotel, asking for autographs.» The fans were not an army, but they crowded round so closely that the hotel was surrounded. Ask: what word would fit here? Crowding round would fit, so the meaning is right.
fact: Besieging means surrounding a town, castle or fort with an army to make it give up.
fact: Besieging can also mean crowding round someone or something, or sending lots of requests.
fact: The besieging army camps outside the walls, cuts off food and water, and waits.
fact: The forms of the verb are besiege, besieges, besieged and besieging.
fact: Phrases to remember: the besieging army and the besieging forces.
exercise: «The besieging army camped outside the walls for months.» What does besieging mean here? | Building a wall to keep the town safe | Cheering for the people inside the town | *Surrounding the town to make it give up | Selling food to the people inside
exercise: «Fans were besieging the stars' hotel, asking for photos.» What does besieging mean here? | Leaving the town in a hurry | Building a new hotel | Sleeping quietly in the hotel | *Crowding round and sending lots of requests
exercise: «The enemy are besieging the castle now.» Find and copy the -ing word. | enemy | *besieging | now | castle
exercise: «The besieging forces cut off food and water.» What is the besieging army trying to do? | Leave the town alone | Build a new road for the town | *Make the people inside give up | Hold a party with the townspeople
exercise: Which phrase is used for an army that surrounds a castle? | The sleeping village | The cheering crowd | The quiet river | *The besieging army
test: «The soldiers besieged the fort for a whole winter.» What is the verb form here? | Besieges | Besiege | *Besieged | Besieging
test: «Crowds of fans were besieging the singer's car after the concert.» What does besieging suggest? | Sleeping under a tree | Running away from the car | A quiet, empty street | *Crowding round, pushing to get close
exam: Which sentence uses besieging to mean crowding round someone? | The river ran slowly past the old mill. | The soldiers rested by the river all day. | The besieging army camped outside the walls for months. | *Fans were besieging the hotel, asking for photos.
